---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PngResizeEvidenceLab from '../components/PngResizeEvidenceLab.tsx';
import { generateBreadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/seo';
import { formatPagePath } from '../lib/urls';

const title = 'PNG Resize Transparency Test - Browser Evidence | LocalResizer';
const description =
  'See what browser-based PNG resizing actually does to transparency, edges, text, and file size. Live, repeatable browser evidence generated in your browser; values vary by browser, OS, and device.';
const breadcrumbItems = [
  { href: '/', label: 'Home' },
  { href: formatPagePath('resize-image'), label: 'Resize Image Guide' },
  { href: formatPagePath('png-resize-transparency-test'), label: 'PNG resize transparency test' },
];
const breadcrumbSchema = generateBreadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbItems);
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schemas={[breadcrumbSchema]}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-5 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <div class="text-center mb-8">
      <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-700 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">Evidence</span>
      <h1 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 tracking-tight mb-4">PNG resize transparency test</h1>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-400 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">
        Published and updated <time datetime="2026-07-14">Jul 14, 2026</time>
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        This page runs a small browser experiment with defined, repeatable test patterns. It draws four PNG patterns, resizes each one with the same browser Canvas primitives -- <code class="text-sm bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">drawImage</code> and PNG <code class="text-sm bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">toBlob</code> -- used by our <a href={formatPagePath('resize-png')} class="text-teal-700 hover:text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-2">Resize PNG</a> page, and reports the real dimensions, byte sizes, and transparency state that the current browser produces. The lab is a self-contained implementation; it is not the same code path as <a href={formatPagePath('resize-png')} class="text-teal-700 hover:text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-2">Resize PNG</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-7 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 mb-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">What the test measures</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        Browser-based PNG resizing is often described as "keeping transparency," but that statement is incomplete. The alpha channel can remain present while the actual pixel resampling changes edges, text, and fine detail. This lab makes those changes visible and measurable.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        For each pattern the lab reports:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-stone-500 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li>Source and output pixel dimensions.</li>
        <li>Source and output PNG byte sizes from <code class="text-sm bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">canvas.toBlob('image/png')</code>.</li>
        <li>Signed byte-size percentage change.</li>
        <li>Whether fully transparent pixels exist in the source and output.</li>
        <li>Whether semi-transparent pixels exist in the source and output.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        The numbers are not hard-coded. They come from the browser you are using right now, so they can differ across Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and operating-system versions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-7 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 mb-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Methodology and scope</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        Every pattern is generated from scratch on an in-memory canvas. Test inputs are drawn in memory rather than read from an uploaded user image, image content is not uploaded for processing, and metrics are not pre-filled. The source canvas is cleared to transparent, the pattern is drawn, and the canvas is exported as PNG. The output canvas is created at the selected percentage with image smoothing enabled and <code class="text-sm bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">imageSmoothingQuality</code> set to <code class="text-sm bg-stone-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">high</code>; the browser decides how to apply that request, so results can vary.
      </p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        The four patterns cover the cases most likely to change during resize:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-stone-500 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Transparent logo edges:</strong> opaque shapes on a transparent background, where resampling creates new semi-transparent pixels along edges.</li>
        <li><strong>Text and UI lines:</strong> small text and card-style rectangles that test crispness at small sizes.</li>
        <li><strong>Semi-transparent gradient:</strong> alpha gradients and soft shadows that remain partially transparent through resize.</li>
        <li><strong>Fine line art:</strong> thin diagonal strokes and grids that are sensitive to pixel mixing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-7 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Limitations</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-stone-500 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li>The test uses synthetic patterns, not your own files. It demonstrates browser behavior, not a prediction for every real image.</li>
        <li>Byte counts and resampling details vary by browser, OS, and device pixel ratio. Do not treat one run as a universal benchmark.</li>
        <li>The lab does not test enlargement, cropping, format conversion, or compression beyond the resize step.</li>
        <li>Patterns are generated at a fixed source size. Very large or very small source images may behave differently.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="mb-10">
      <PngResizeEvidenceLab client:visible />
    </div>

    <section class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-7 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 mb-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-xl font-semibold text-stone-900">How to read the results</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        A smaller PNG is expected after downscaling, but the exact byte reduction depends on pattern complexity and the browser encoder. Watch for two specific signals:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-stone-500 leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Semi-transparent pixels appear in the output when they were absent in the source.</strong> This is normal resampling along edges. It does not mean transparency is broken, but it does mean the edge is now softer.</li>
        <li><strong>Fine lines become thicker or thinner.</strong> When a pattern is downscaled, the browser blends neighboring pixels. Hairline details may dim, thicken, or disappear.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">
        Use this evidence to decide whether a smaller dimension is acceptable for your use case. If the destination requires pixel-perfect edges, keep the source larger. If the destination only needs a compact preview, the resized output is usually fine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      <a href={formatPagePath('resize-png')} class="no-underline bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 hover:border-teal-200 hover:bg-teal-50/40 transition-all">
        <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-2">Resize PNG</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">Open the live PNG resizer and apply the same browser-local workflow to your own transparent PNG files.</p>
      </a>
      <a href={formatPagePath('resize-image')} class="no-underline bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-soft border border-stone-100 hover:border-teal-200 hover:bg-teal-50/40 transition-all">
        <h2 class="font-[var(--font-heading)] text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-2">Resize image guide</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">Compare the PNG resizer with batch resize, target-size pages, and other current resize workflows.</p>
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
